{
  "design": {
    "theme": "studio",
    "picture_style": "photo"
  },
  "flow": [
    {
      "slide": 1,
      "does": "Recall gas collection and evidence of a chemical reaction",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "picture",
        "shows": "Bubbles forming as magnesium reacts with acid"
      },
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "does": "Introduce the approved learning objectives",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "does": "Predict the effect of concentration",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "does": "Teach how to collect and measure hydrogen",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "A sealed flask connected to a gas syringe"
      },
      "teaches": [2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 5,
      "does": "Teach a fair and safe investigation",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "does": "Explain concentration and correct the faster-particles misconception",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Different numbers of acid particles near equal magnesium surfaces"
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "does": "Model a mean-rate calculation",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 8,
      "does": "Check a near-identical mean-rate calculation",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 9,
      "does": "Interpret gas-volume curves and link evidence to collision theory",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Gas volume against time for two acid concentrations"
      },
      "teaches": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 10,
      "does": "Check the explanation for a higher reaction rate",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 11,
      "does": "Apply calculations and evaluate an unfair investigation",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [1, 2, 3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 12,
      "does": "Prepare an independent investigation plan before the worksheet exit ticket",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [2]
    }
  ],
  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "Rates of reaction",
    "lead": "Recall: which gas forms when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid? How could we collect it?",
    "picture": {
      "shows": "Magnesium ribbon reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid in a laboratory flask",
      "must_see": [
        "magnesium ribbon",
        "colourless acid",
        "bubbles around magnesium"
      ],
      "subject": "generic"
    }
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "discussion",
      "heading": "Predict the change",
      "lead": "Two flasks contain equal masses of magnesium and equal volumes of hydrochloric acid at the same temperature. One acid is twice as concentrated. Predict which produces 20 cm³ of gas first. Explain your prediction.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Measure the hydrogen",
      "lead": "Magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen",
      "points": [
        {
          "label": "Measure",
          "text": "Read gas volume in cm³ at regular times."
        },
        {
          "label": "Seal",
          "text": "A gas-tight connection prevents hydrogen escaping."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "labelled-diagram",
        "shows": "A conical flask containing hydrochloric acid and magnesium, sealed by a bung with a delivery tube leading to a horizontally clamped graduated gas syringe. Show a stopwatch alongside, with no flame.",
        "labels": [
          "Gas syringe",
          "Clamp",
          "Delivery tube",
          "Bung",
          "Hydrochloric acid",
          "Magnesium",
          "Stopwatch"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "steps",
      "heading": "Plan a fair investigation",
      "points": [
        "Use equal acid volumes at different known concentrations. Keep acid in excess.",
        "Use equal masses of similar magnesium ribbon. Keep the starting temperature the same.",
        "Add magnesium, fit the bung promptly and start timing. Use the same procedure each time.",
        "Record gas volume every 10 s until it stops changing. Repeat each concentration and calculate mean volumes.",
        "Wear goggles; avoid skin contact with acid. Keep hydrogen away from flames."
      ],
      "figure": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "More particles, not faster particles",
      "lead": "At the same temperature, higher concentration does not make particles move faster.",
      "points": [
        "More acid particles occupy each cm³.",
        "Collisions with magnesium happen more often.",
        "More successful collisions occur each second."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "particles",
        "shows": "Two equal-volume panels at the same temperature, each beside an identical magnesium surface. Show fewer acid particles in the dilute panel and more in the concentrated panel. Use equal-length motion arrows in both. Acid particles are a simplified model of the reacting particles in solution.",
        "labels": [
          "Lower concentration",
          "Higher concentration",
          "Same temperature"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "equation-hero",
      "heading": "Calculate a mean rate",
      "lead": "A reaction produces 48 cm³ of hydrogen in 40 s, starting from 0 cm³.",
      "formula": "Mean rate = gas volume ÷ time",
      "points": [
        "Mean rate = 48 ÷ 40",
        "Mean rate = 1.2 cm³/s",
        "This is the average over the first 40 s."
      ],
      "figure": null
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Your turn: mean rate",
      "questions": [
        "A reaction produces 54 cm³ of hydrogen in 45 s, starting from 0 cm³. Calculate its mean rate.",
        "What does your answer mean in terms of gas produced each second, on average?"
      ],
      "instruction": "Write the formula, substitution and answer with units.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Read the evidence",
      "lead": "Equal magnesium masses; acid in excess; same temperature. Calculate each mean rate over 20 s. Explain the difference.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "Hydrogen volume against time for equal magnesium masses reacting with equal volumes of two hydrochloric acid concentrations at the same temperature",
        "alt": "At 20 seconds, 0.5 mol/dm³ acid produces 20 cm³ and 1.0 mol/dm³ produces 40 cm³. Both eventually reach 60 cm³.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {
          "label": "Time (s)",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "step": 20
        },
        "y": {
          "label": "Hydrogen volume (cm³)",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 60,
          "step": 10
        },
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": false,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "0.5 mol/dm³",
            "points": [[0, 0], [10, 11], [20, 20], [30, 29], [40, 37], [50, 44], [60, 50], [70, 55], [80, 58], [90, 60], [100, 60]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          },
          {
            "label": "1.0 mol/dm³",
            "points": [[0, 0], [10, 23], [20, 40], [30, 52], [40, 58], [50, 60], [60, 60], [70, 60], [80, 60], [90, 60], [100, 60]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": null,
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "hinge",
      "heading": "Choose the best explanation",
      "stem": "Why does more concentrated acid react faster at the same temperature?",
      "options": [
        "Acid particles move faster.",
        "More successful collisions occur each second.",
        "Every collision now causes a reaction.",
        "The magnesium gains more surface area."
      ],
      "correct": 2
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Evaluate a new investigation",
      "questions": [
        "Test A: 0.5 mol/dm³ acid, 0.05 g magnesium, 20°C; 24 cm³ gas in 30 s. Test B: 1.0 mol/dm³ acid, 0.10 g magnesium, 30°C; 48 cm³ in 30 s. Calculate both mean rates from 0 cm³.",
        "Both use 25 cm³ acid. Can these results show the effect of concentration alone? Explain and identify two changes needed for a fair comparison.",
        "Stretch: with equal magnesium masses and temperature, explain why higher concentration can produce gas faster without producing more gas overall. Assume acid is in excess."
      ],
      "instruction": "Answer independently. Use evidence and scientific reasons.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Design the comparison",
      "questions": [
        "You have 0.5 and 1.0 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid, magnesium ribbon and a gas syringe. Describe how to collect gas-volume readings over time.",
        "State how you will control magnesium mass and starting temperature, and make the results more reliable."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write a brief method on your worksheet.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
